WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.590
Hollywood can never be totally sure what audiences
will like, and with every mega-hit and critical

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:09.720
darling, there come a few more shows doomed
to a quick cancellation.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.670
According to the critics, these are the worst
shows to hit broadcast TV, cable, and streaming

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:15.870
services in 2019.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:21.859
Network TV's biggest breakout hit over the
last three years or so is unquestionably This

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.410
Is Us, NBC's sprawling, time-jumping, touching
family tearjerker.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:31.300
As that formula has proven successful, other
similar shows have hit the airwaves, including

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.260
NBC's own The Village.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.899
Rather than focus on a family, it's an attempt
at a poignant, heartwarming and emotionally

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:42.059
devastating show about the diverse residents
of a Brooklyn apartment building.

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:47.030
It's about how everyone's lives interact in
ways both subtle and profound, and feels specifically

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.449
designed to get audiences to cry.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.320
Dustin Rowles of Pajiba says:

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.059
"The Village has no interest in being a decent
series; its only interest is in using emotional

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:01.269
manipulation to gaslight its viewers into
believing that The Village is trying to say

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.800
something profound about the human condition."

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.560
More than 15 years after the release of Bad
Boys II, the franchise returned to life with

00:01:09.579 --> 00:01:13.420
a made-for-TV spinoff starring Gabrielle Union
and Jessica Alba.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:19.350
They play an LAPD detective team who are,
get this, very different, personality-wise.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.759
In short, L.A.'s Finest embraces a formula
that's been used countless times before in

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:25.590
movies and TV.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.870
Critics were savage, finding the end results
ordinary, outdated, and unnecessary.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:35.200
Ben Travers of IndieWire criticized the standard
storytelling in his review:

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.149
"Everyone can find another show exactly like
this one on Fox or other broadcast networks,

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:44.030
USA or other cable outlets, and even streaming
services."

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.560
"Don't make me shoot you!

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.680
Because I'm not missing family book club."

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.520
After American Idol became a top-rated pop
culture juggernaut in the early 2000s, talent

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.200
shows flooded TV.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.429
In 2019, CBS used its valuable post-Super
Bowl programming slot to launch yet another

00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:03.329
entry in the genre: The World's Best.

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:07.659
In some ways, the James Corden-hosted show
is formulaic and familiar.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:12.200
Like The Voice and American Idol, performers
compete in front of a panel of celebrities.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.049
And like America's Got Talent, acts of every
stripe are welcome, not just singers.

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:21.310
But in its attempt to flip the script and
get innovative, The World's Best goes too

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.190
far and just winds up overly complicated.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:28.870
In addition to doing their thing for the judges,
performers are rated by up to 50 experts from

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:33.709
38 geographic regions, purporting to be experts
in different forms of entertainment.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:39.220
Then, all the various musicians, comedians,
karate masters, etc. compete against each

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.680
other for the title of "The World's Best"
overall new talent.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.209
Caroline Framke, writing at Variety wrote:

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.909
"So very much of the show boils down to the
show explaining itself and stretching out

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.240
the 'judging,' which mostly just consists
of the panelists gushing and gasping in awe."

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.680
What if Netflix made a TV show that played
like a cheesy '90s-style erotic thriller?

00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:04.349
And what if they got an Academy Award-winning
actress to star in it?

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:09.709
The answer to questions is What / If, a steamy
primetime soap from Netflix about a high-powered

00:03:09.709 --> 00:03:13.430
and glamorous Silicon Valley investor played
by Renée Zellweger.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:18.100
In between her time spent screaming her frustrations
into a stuffed animal and practicing archery

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:24.159
on human targets, she helps out Lisa, an idealistic
young head of a biotech company in exchange

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:26.489
for a night with the woman's husband, Sean.

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:30.600
The whole thing is such an inscrutable mess
that David Opie of Digital Spy wrote:

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.959
"There's a strong chance that the team behind
What / If know exactly what they're doing,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.600
deliberately setting out to make something
that's intrinsically 'bad.'"

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.709
Katee Sackhoff, the breakout star of the classic
2000s reboot of Battlestar Galactica makes

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:49.659
her triumphant return to sci-fi television
with Another Life, a tense Netflix drama about

00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:53.760
a spaceship full of humans on its way to make
first contact with aliens.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.760
Unfortunately for Galactica fans, Another
Life is not quite the worthy follow-up they

00:03:58.780 --> 00:03:59.780
wanted.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.250
This ambitious show is part workplace drama,
as starship commander Niko Breckenridge looks

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:09.390
after a craft headed to a far-away alien planet
which sent a giant, creepy crystal to Earth.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:14.000
Giving her plenty of guff is her number-two,
Ian, the now-demoted former captain of that

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.000
very ship.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Awkward.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.790
Meanwhile, her husband back on Earth is the
only man who can crack the code of the giant

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:21.140
creepy crystal.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:25.780
There's a lot going on with Another Life,
but according to Alex McLevy of The AV Club,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.960
it doesn't all work:

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.170
"Another Life is an unwieldy mashup of Arrival
and any number of space-odyssey adventure

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:36.750
serials, and struggles to maintain clarity
or depth with either half of its bifurcated

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.040
storyline.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.400
While this CW summer science-fiction series
shares a name with another famous science-fiction

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.940
property, Pandora actually takes place primarily
on Earth.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.960
Nevertheless, it cribs heavily from plenty
of other popular sci-fi.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.790
Pandora is a Star Trek-esque series set at
the space travel training ground of Starfleet

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:58.190
Academy in everything but name.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:03.500
It begins in 2199 on a Tatooine-like space
world called New Portland, where a college-age

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:07.030
woman named Jax is the only survivor of a
devastating attack.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:11.330
So, she's shipped off to Fleet Training Academy
on Earth, where she'll study under the tutelage

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:15.200
of her uncle alongside a number of familiar
sci-fi character archetypes.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.310
All the while, Jax may have a special secret
which may affect the very future of humankind.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.840
Lightweight and exceedingly familiar can be
fine, especially for a summer TV show.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.100
But Pandora doesn't quite strike that tone.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.760
Beth Elderkin of io9 was exceedingly harsh
in her review:

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.760
"I watch a lot of TV pilots for work, including
ones for shows that never make it to air,

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:40.580
and Pandora's is quite possibly one of the
worst I've seen in years."

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.600
"I'm not sure we're completely getting off
on the right foot here, are we?"

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.200
"Well, it appears I'm not the only one that's
perceptive then."

